<attribute name="Ypos" >0</attribute>
<text >The Motivational Vision for Introducing Tinderbox Packages
The core concept for Tinderbox Packages is to be able to bundle up a collection of notes that can then be dropped into any Tinderbox document so that the collection behaves as intended—i.e., the collection works the same way in the new document as it did in the original document. While nearly everything in Tinderbox is represented by a note, notes may also need user attributes and link types, so these need to be part of the package. In addition, for some types of notes to be usable, they need to be placed into specific locations within a Tinderbox document, so deploying a package needs to handle placement of each note type into the appropriate location. By automating all of this with a standard approach to assembling a package, it should be much easier for users to replicate features developed in one document into their other documents.
A Package can be just a simple collection of notes that a user wants to have as a starting point for a new document or project. Such simple collections might include prototypes, templates, user attributes, and maybe link types. Packages could also include automation using code, including stamps, functions, and various rules. More elaborate packages might provide a complete application built within Tinderbox, or a set of extensions to allow Tinderbox to interoperate with other applications.
To fulfill this vision, various requirements need to be considered along with the approaches for meeting each requirement. These requirements and approaches are presented in the following sections:
Portability
It should be possible to drop a Package into any Tinderbox document while maintaining all useful features of the Package’s collection. The Package itself should be a standalone container that can be copied and pasted into any destination document as a single procedure. At the same time, deploying a Package into a Tinderbox document should not adversely affect content already included in the document. While conflicts may occur, these should be resolved in a non-destructive manner that allows recovery of conflicting material.  
Consistency
Packages should employ common practices for placement of shared elements, including documentation about the Package. For Packages that include action code, use of standardized code libraries should be supported. Such code libraries could be included within a Package, or preferably as a separate Package on which other Packages can rely. To achieve consistency, a framework must be established that defines where to locate shared information in a document, as well as any common tools necessary to support Package deployments.
Minimalist approach
The Package framework must be lightweight in nature and unobtrusive within a user’s document. It should not add unnecessary bulk to a document. Any code associated with the framework should either be quiescent or designed to add only negligible computational overhead. There should be clear rules for what can be pruned from the framework, such as copies of documentation.
No-code assembly
Any Tinderbox user should be able to assemble their own Packages without having to do any coding. Similarly, the Package deployment process should be fully automated without requiring any effort by a Package user. The assembly process should only require collecting the various notes to be included in the Package, and placing copies of these notes into the appropriate Package Manifest containers. The Package’s Manifest will be defined by the collection itself. 
Sharable
Packages should be easily shared between users.  Aside: It must be noted that sharing of packages between users presents inherent security risks (see below).
Coexistence
Multiple Packages should be able to coexist in the same document, even if the Packages are provided by different users. By establishing a common framework for supporting deployment and use of Packages, potential conflicts between packages provided by different authors can be eliminated.
Updatability
When items in a Package get updated, it should be possible to incorporate these updates into any document where the Package had been previously deployed by merely re-deploying the updated Package. Any newer items should replace older items, but without deleting the older version.
Maintainability
Problems will emerge with use of Packages. These problems might relate to how a Package is deployed, or as a result of conflicts with other packages. A Package might require new features in Tinderbox, and so might not work with older Tinderbox versions. Future updates to Tinderbox (or other external tools) might adversely affect behavior of a Package. And, of course, there can be logic errors or coding bugs. For any of these potential problems, it will be important to have standard ways to analyze behavior and isolate the causes of any problems. Consequently, the Package framework should include debugging tools and the ability to capture information about potential misbehavior. This is especially important for Package deployment operations.
Safety
Deploying a Package needs to be a safe procedure that does not delete or otherwise destroy any items within the destination document. However, Tinderbox provides a robust environment that can access data from multiple sources, and potentially change or delete information outside the confines of the Tinderbox document. Tools will be needed to audit ways that Tinderbox Packages might adversely affect the contents within a document, and contents accessed outside the document’s boundaries. At the very least, users need to be made aware of any data safety issues related to Packages they deploy and use.
Security
For all the benefits of Packages and the ability to share Packages, there are very real concerns about how these benefits could be unintentionally or intentionally turned into threats. This opens Pandora’s Box, and many difficult issues will need to be addressed sooner rather than later. This will quickly become the greatest challenge for Tinderbox Package Management. The trick will be to convert mitigation measures into useful benefits for the Tinderbox community.</text>
