<text >The library container holds notes that define functions — actions that appear can be used in other Tinderbox actions. For example, you might write
	function triple(x)
		 {return(3*x);}
to allow you to write expressions such as &apos;triple(5)&apos;.
	You can define many functions in the same library note, and you can create different library notes for different tasks.
Library note names that begin with a parentheses, like this note, are treated as commentary or documentation. Other names may reflect the intent of the functions the note contains.</text>
