<attribute name="Subtitle" >User Attribute Management</attribute>
<attribute name="Xpos" >14</attribute>
<attribute name="Ypos" >0</attribute>
<text >The “User Attribute Management” package has been designed and implemented to minimize the effort required to deploy this package to a new or existing Tinderbox document. 
The procedures outlined below copy from a source Tinderbox document (likely this very document) to a destination document that may be an existing document or a newly created document. None of these procedures will modify the content of an existing destination document, but will add new content to facilitate managing User Attributes.

Step 1: Establish Tinderbox Hints
If the destination for this package is a new Tinderbox document, invoke “Built-in Hints” from the Tinderbox File menu.
If the destination is an existing document, confirm that there is a “Hints” folder at the top level. If not, invoke “Built-in Hints” from the File menu. 
Step 2: Add “Document Commons” container to source document
From the source document, locate the “Commons Template” container within the “Document Commons” top level container. Then, select and copy the “Document Commons” template. Note this should be the container that resides in the Commons Template. 
￼
In the destination document, paste the “Document Commons” template. It doesn’t matter where it is located, since it will move on its own to be the last top-level note in the document. It will bring with it the “Global Settings” and “Document Attributes” template containers, and potentially other templates as well.
Note: While it is possible, and potentially desirable, to copy the actual “Document Commons” container at the top level of the source document, this does run the risk of adding unintended stuff to the destination document. There is nothing but structure and documentation in the template, so this is safer to copy.
Aside: I usually turn the top-level containers used to hold the document’s infrastructure (e.g., prototypes, templates, code, global data, technical documentation, etc.) into separators. Not only does this help visually recognize stuff in the outline view, it also hides this infrastructure clutter from other views, especially the Map view. 
Step 3: Activate the “Document Commons” container
Select the “Document Commons” container that was just copied into the destination document, and uncheck the $RuleDisabled box (i.e., set $RuleDisabled to false). This will cause the “Document Commons” container to position itself as the last top-level note in the source document, and the Rule will continue to maintain this position.
Note: if there happens to already be a note or container in the last top-level position with the same Name, then an error message will be displayed, and the Rule will be disabled. This is to prevent having a situation where multiple copies of the same container chase each other for last place.
Although optional, it is recommended that the $EdictDisabled checkbox be unchecked so that the Edict for this “Document Commons” container will run. This builds a dictionary that can be used to find children of this container without having to know the name of this parent container. This provides support for other packages.
Step 4: Copy Code for Edict and OnAdd actions
In the destination document, turn off (uncheck) $RuleDisabled for the “Document Attributes” container located within the “Document Commons” container. This will move two action code notes into the “/Hints” container, and update them so they can be automatically executed. Since this Rule only needs to run once, it will set $RuleDisabled back to true (checked on) just before completing its execution.
This completes the basic setup for maintaining and copying User Attributes between Tinderbox documents.
For the curious, the two Action Code notes that were moved into the “/Hints” container are:
	1.	Edict (Attribute Note) The Attribute Notes use this action code note for their Edict that will execute periodically and whenever the Attribute Note is modified.
	2.	OnVisit (Document Attributes) The “Document Attributes” container invokes this code via its $OnAdd rule. Whenever the “Document Attributes” container is selected (i.e., ‘visited’), this code will rescan all User Attributes in the current document. If a User Attribute does not have an Attribute Note in the “Document Attributes” container, then one will be created for it. The current settings for all User Attributes will be copied into the corresponding Attribute Notes.
Step 5: Rescan User Attributes and update all Attribute Notes
In the destination document, turn on $Checked for the “Document Attributes” container. This will enable the $OnVisit action code that will scan all User Attributes in the document, and update the Attribute Notes in this container to reflect the current settings for each User Attribute. If a User Attribute is not already represented by an Attribute Note, then this will be created. (It may be necessary to select another note, and then reselect the “Document Attributes” container to execute the OnVisit code.)
Note: The rescan and update will take place each time the “Document Attributes” container is visited (i.e., selected). However, the OnVisit code will only run if $Checked is currently true. Hence, if this rescan and update behavior is not desired, turn off $Checked.
Note: It is not necessary to perform this step if all that is desired is to copy one or more User Attributes from another document. However, it is recommended that this be done anyway so that any potential duplicates or other conflicts can be recognized before bringing in new User Attributes.
Step 6: Copy Attribute Notes from source to destination document
At this point, everything should be ready to maintain User Attributes and facilitate easily moving User Attributes between documents. This can be done in bulk, or on an ad hoc basis with just one or two User Attributes at a time. All that is required is that each document (source and destination) must have completed these steps to establish the “Document Commons” container with the subordinate “Document Attributes” container. User Attributes will be copied and pasted as Attribute Notes found in the “Document Attributes” container in each document. 
The procedure to copy User Attributes from one document to another merely requires selecting from the source document the Attribute Notes under the “Document Attributes” container that should be copied. Then invoke “copy” (e.g., ^C) for the selected notes, go to the destination document, and paste the copied Attribute Notes into the “Document Attributes” container. The corresponding User Attributes will be added to the destination document automatically without further user interaction.
</text>
