<attribute name="Sentiments" >0;-0.2;-0.8;-0.8;-1;-0.6;-0.8;-0.6;0.2;-0.6;-0.6;-0.6;0.4;-1;-0.2;-1;-0.4;0.2;-0.6;-0.4;0;-0.6;0.4;0.4;0;0.6;0.6;-0.6;0</attribute>
<attribute name="Subtitle" >User Attribute Management</attribute>
<attribute name="Xpos" >17.5</attribute>
<attribute name="Ypos" >0</attribute>
<text >Design objectives: 
	•	Maintain a set of notes that directly correspond to each User Attribute in the associated Tinderbox document. Each such note is referred to as an “Attribute Note” (though only used for User Attributes).
	•	Automatically produce the collection of Attribute Notes that correspond to each User Attribute in the associated document. The resulting Attribute Notes will have the corresponding User Attribute settings reflected into System Attributes for the Note. 
	•	Minimize dependencies on anything in the residing document. In particular, this package does not require any User Attributes to be predefined. Similarly, no prototypes, stamps, or functions are required. All code will be executed as actions attached to Attribute Notes and the corresponding parent “Document Attributes” container.
	•	Attribute Notes can be edited with the resulting changes reflected back into corresponding User Attribute specifications.
	•	Attribute Notes can be referenced elsewhere in the Tinderbox document as part of any technical or usage documentation. For example, action code (e.g., stamps, library functions) might have links to any Attribute Notes that correspond to User Attributes actually used by the code. 
Implementation Details: 
	•	Additions to a Tinderbox document’s structure include:
	⁃	A container typically named “Document Commons” that will be established as the last top-level note in the document. It is recommended that this container be copied from the provided template container since this will include a Rule to force this container to always be the last top-level note in outline order. In addition, an Edict is used to build a dictionary that can be easily referenced from anywhere in the overall Tinderbox document to access the children of this Document Commons container. Further documentation is available in the $Text for this container. 
	⁃	A container named “Document Attributes” that will contain the Attribute Notes. This should be placed within the “Document Commons” container, which allows its $ID to be retrieved using this code:    $MyDictionary(lastSibling(cover))[&quot;Document Attributes&quot;] The template for the Document Attributes container includes an “OnVisit” action:    action($Text(&quot;/Hints/OnVisit (Document Attributes)&quot;)); …that will initially build the complete set of Attribute Notes within this container, and subsequently update the set of Attribute Notes to overwrite their copies of the User Attribute settings while also adding Attribute Notes for any missing from the complete set of User Attributes. Note that the $OnAdd code will only execute when $Checked is true (check mark showing).
	•	Reflecting Attribute Notes back into the corresponding User Attribute settings is accomplished via an Edict defined for each Attribute Note.
	⁃	The Edict invokes action code stored in “/Hints/Edict (Attribute Note)” as:    action($Text(&quot;/Hints/Edict (Attribute Note)&quot;));
	⁃	The default behavior is that only the contents of $Text will be used to update the “description” of the corresponding User Attribute. This allows the user to edit descriptions using the Attribute Note text pane instead of utilizing the more constrained method of editing descriptions using the Inspector.
	⁃	If the $Checked boolean is set to true for an Attribute Note, then the ‘default,’ ‘suggested,’ and ‘lines’ Attribute settings will also be updated from the associated System Attributes (i.e., $MyString, $MySet, $MyNumber).
	⁃	By setting the $EdictDisabled boolean to true (or clicking the Inspector’s Edict “Enabled” box off), execution of the Edict to update the corresponding User Attribute can be disabled, thereby preventing changes made to the Attribute Note being applied to the User Attribute settings.	
Mappings: 
Attribute Notes utilize pre-defined System Attributes to store the settings for the associated User Attribute. This table provides the mappings:
	System 	User Attribute	
	Attribute	Setting	Description
	$Subtitle	type	A string representing the attribute type
	$MyBoolean	sequential	If the type is “number,” then this option enables sequential default value assignments
	$MyString	default	Values to assign to Attribute by default
	$MySet	suggested	A list of values that can be selected from a pop-up list
	$MyNumber	lines	For some Attribute types, the number of lines to be shown in the Displayed Attributes pane is given here
	$Text	description	A user-provided description of the User Attribute. It is often easier to edit descriptions in the Text pane than using the Inspector. 
Terminology: 
	•	Attribute Note: A note that reflects a specific User Attribute in the associated Tinderbox document (one-to-one correspondence). 
	•	Attribute settings: These are the configuration settings for both System and User Attributes. 
</text>
