<item ID="1750360919" Creator="Chuck Wade" >
<attribute name="Badge" >next</attribute>
<attribute name="Created" >2025-02-25T17:25:02+09:00</attribute>
<attribute name="DisplayedAttributes" >Creator;Modified;Subtitle</attribute>
<attribute name="DominantLanguage" >en</attribute>
<attribute name="Email" >Chuck@Interisle.net</attribute>
<attribute name="Modified" >2025-06-19T14:57:02+09:00</attribute>
<attribute name="Name" >ReadMe “Debugging &amp; Logging Tools”</attribute>
<attribute name="ScreenHeight" >22</attribute>
<attribute name="ScreenWidth" >679</attribute>
<attribute name="SelectionCount" >47</attribute>
<attribute name="Sentiment" >-0.2000000055</attribute>
<attribute name="Sentiments" >0;-0.6;0.8;-0.4;-0.2;-0.8</attribute>
<attribute name="Subtitle" >Debugging &amp; Logging Tools</attribute>
<attribute name="Telephone" >+1 508 277-6439</attribute>
<attribute name="Xpos" >17.5</attribute>
<attribute name="Ypos" >0</attribute>
<text >Installation of the Debugging and Logging Tools is now required for some of the packages that are provided as options for users to install in their Tinderbox documents. The reason for requiring these tools is that it allows a package to be provided with debug statements included in the associated code, though usually under the control of a “debug” switch that is normally turned off. However, if problems are encountered with a package, it is possible to turn on debugging, and provide the package developer with log results that can help pinpoint a problem. These debug logs can also help a package user analyze the code and learn how the package works. This might help users customize a package for their own purposes.

Additional documentation for how to use the Debugging and Logging Tools package can be found in this note:
/Document Commons/Packages/Debugging &amp; Logging Tools/Documentation

Feedback is welcome. 
Chuck Wade &lt;Chuck@Interisle.net&gt; +1 508 277-6439
</text>
