<attribute name="Subtitle" >Debugging &amp; Logging Tools</attribute>
<attribute name="Width" >3</attribute>
<attribute name="Xpos" >3.5</attribute>
<attribute name="Ypos" >0</attribute>
<text >The ability to have code generate log entries in log books, computer files, and even to remote “syslog” servers became an essential technique long ago in software development and systems operation. Today, logging represents a vital part of modern software/system ecosystems.  This Tinderbox toolbox document introduces a reasonably robust and flexible logging facility that can be incorporated into any Tinderbox document.  This logging facility can be used in debugging and testing all types of code within Tinderbox documents, as well as for recording significant events or alerts, such as errors, warnings, or status changes. It is offered to the community of Tinderbox users without any encumbrances.
The logging tools provided in this Tinderbox document (version 9.1 or later is required) comprise the following elements:
	1.	A function, LogRec(logbook, entry), that enters a supplied log entry into the note that is referenced as the logbook to be used. This function is stored in the /Hints/Library/ as “Record Log Entry.” Note that this is the only required code to implement logging. 
	2.	A globally accessible container named “LogBooks” located in the top level container labeled “Document Commons” (displayed as a separator in outline view, and not displayed in map view).
	3.	A log record index that is incremented every time the LogRec( ) function is called (except for the “blank” option, see below). This index is kept in $MyNumber(&quot;/Document Commons/LogBooks&quot;)—i.e., in the parent container for all log books. The index allows unambiguous time sequence ordering of log records across all log books. For example, if  three entries are added to a log book named “Log-1” and then two log entries are added to another log book named “Log-2” and then two more log entries are added to Log-1, then the log entries in Log-1 will be indexed as (01, 02, 03, 06, 07) while the indexes for entries in Log-2 would be (04, 05).
	4.	A Prototype labeled “Log Book” that establishes the default configuration of Notes used to receive log entries—i.e., “log books.” The “LogBooks” container will set each new child (on-add) to use the “Log Book” prototype. This Prototype can be modified to specify the font, font size, and paragraph/line spacing for records recorded in log books. 
	5.	A Prototype labeled “Persistent Log Book.” This is equivalent to the “Log Book” Prototype, except that any log book using this Prototype will not be deleted by the “Logging:Delete Log Books” Stamp.
	6.	The actual log books (child Notes) that will be created on the fly, but within the “/Document Commons/LogBooks” container. Each log book can be manually modified to specify how log records appear. If the Prototype is set to “Persistent Log Book” for any log book, then that log book will not be deleted by the “Logging:Delete Log Books” Stamp.
	7.	Several stamps that appear as sub-menu items of “Logging” under the Stamps menu. These currently provide the means to stop/pause all logging, enter a new ad hoc log record or bookmark on the fly, restart indexing, and delete all current log books that do not use the “Persistent Log Book” prototype.
The intended use is that Action code anywhere in the Tinderbox document will call the LogRec( ) function supplying two arguments: (1) the name of a log book, and (2) the text that should be entered as a new log record in the indicated log book. If the supplied logbook argument does not currently exist as a Tinderbox note, then it will be created on the fly as a new child note under the “/Document Commons/LogBooks/” Container with its $Prototype set to “Log Book.” This is the normal way that log books get created, though it is certainly possible to manually create a log book if desired, but it should be a child of the “LogBooks” container.
When the LogRec( ) function sees that there is no current log book note matching the name supplied as the first argument, then it will not only create a new log book with the supplied name, but it will also enter as the first line of $Text the log book’s name followed by a time and date stamp indicating when it was created. This is so that when copying all the $Text out of this note, there will be a header that states the name and creation time for the log. 
The log entry supplied as the second argument to the LogRec( ) function can be a number, or a text string, or a calculated result. In practice, it is something that should be tracked or that can be used to debug working code. Each log entry will be appended to the current $Text attribute in the referenced log book so that multiple calls to LogRec( ) referencing the same log book will result in a sequence of log entries being recorded, one per line. Some example use cases:
	•	When debugging code, the LogRec( ) function can be used to record current values of variables, or indicators of what path has been taken within the code. This can provide useful details that will aid in finding and fixing coding mistakes, or testing that unusual or edge conditions are handled correctly. Code execution logging is an essential capability if practicing defensive coding where evidence is presented that the code executes according to the design.
	•	If it is desirable to track progress or significant events within a Tinderbox document, such events can be logged along with any relevant details. For example, if a Tinderbox document pulls information from other external sources, such activity can be logged with details on whether information was successfully retrieved, or not. Similarly, log entries can be made when certain milestone conditions are reached, or certain activities are initiated by the user. 
	•	By allowing each LogRec( ) instance to specify the destination log book that should be used (credit to Mark Bernstein for suggesting this feature), it is feasible to simultaneously support logging for different activities. For example, debugging logs can go to one log book, while log records associated with activities being tracked can go to another log book. This significantly expands the utility of this one function. 
Some additional features have been added to the LogRec( ) function that leverage several User Attributes assigned for the “/Document Commons/LogBooks/“ container and that can also be assigned for each child log book. These attributes are:
	•	$LoggingEnabled: A boolean switch that can be used to disable (i.e., unchecked) logging for a specific log book, or globally if set for the LogBooks Container. The “Logging:Stop Logging!” stamp will uncheck —i.e., set to false—$LoggingEnabled(&quot;/Document Commons/LogBooks&quot;), which will stop any further logging to any log book—i.e., any invocations of the LogRec( ) function will do nothing. When a new log book is created on the fly, then $LoggingEnabled will be initialized to true. Subsequently, setting $LoggingEnabled to false for a specific log book will disable further logging for only that particular log book. 
	•	$IndexLogs: If set (checked), then each log entry will be prepended by an index number that is incremented every time LogRec( ) is called from anywhere. Note that, unless the “blank” option is invoked (see below), the index will always be incremented for every call to LogRec( ), and this index value is maintained in $MyNumber(&quot;/Document Commons/LogBooks&quot;)—i.e., in the parent container for all log books. By checking—i.e., setting to true—$IndexLogs for the LogBooks container, indexing can be enabled for each new log book created on the fly. However, the setting for $IndexLogs for a specific log book determines whether or not indexes will be prepended for new log records added. 
	•	$DatestampLogs: Date stamps will be prepended to log entries if this attribute is checked in any specific log book. When new log books are created on the fly, then the value of  $DatestampLogs(/Document Commons/LogBooks) will be copied to the same attribute for the new log book. The date stamp will come after the index, if enabled, and will be in the form YY-MM-DD, which is the preferred date format for logging applications.  
	•	$TimestampLogs: Time stamps will be prepended to log entries if this attribute is checked in any specific log book. When new log books are created on the fly, then the value of $TimestampLogs(/Document Commons/LogBooks) will be copied to the same attribute for the new log book. The time stamp will come after the index and date stamp if either is enabled, and will be in the form HH:MM:SS using a 24-hour clock. As with indexing and date stamps, time stamps can be forcibly enabled for a specific log book by setting this attribute for that log book to true (checked).  Aside: Versions of Tinderbox (prior to 9.2.2b561) will not capture the current time with seconds (i.e., the seconds are always 00). If this capability is needed, then the LogRec( ) function can use a workaround to obtain seconds using the “runCommand” and the Unix “date” command. This workaround is currently commented out, but it can be enabled by removing the initial ‘//’ from this line: //time_stamp.second=runCommand(&quot;date &apos;+%S&apos;&quot;);
It is worth noting that, while newly created log books inherit the current settings for the $IndexLogs, $DatestampLogs, and $TimestampLogs attributes, these can be later changed at will to customize the logging behavior for each individual log book. Similarly, if these settings are modified for the “LogBooks” container, then previously created log books will keep their current settings, but new log books will inherit the revised settings.
Since the current time is returned by the LogRec( ) function, this feature can be used as a convenient way to track the time sequencing of code. For example, this can be used to compute time intervals between different points in code or for external functions, such as capturing a web page. The “end” option (see below) will record a special log entry that includes the interval between the current time and some previously captured time.
The LogRec( ) function can also accept options that change the way that log entries are recorded. An option is specified by prepending “&lt;&lt;option&gt;&gt;” to the beginning of the log entry argument. The options that are currently recognized are:
	•	blank: This will enter a blank or empty line in the specified log book. Except for the “&lt;&lt;blank&gt;&gt;” option specification, the rest of the log entry is ignored. Also, the log index will not be updated, since this action is considered to be whitespace only. Here is how to enter a blank line: 	LogRec(logbook,&quot;&lt;&lt;blank&gt;&gt;&quot;);
	•	mark: A specially formatted bookmark entry can be recorded when this option is specified. The bookmark log record is intended to stand out from other log records, and it always includes the index number and a date/time stamp. To illustrate, here is how to enter a bookmark record: 	LogRec(logbook,&quot;&lt;&lt;mark&gt;&gt;Begin main loop&quot;); This will record a bookmark entry that will look something like this: 	***** Bookmark: Index=00001: 25-04-01 13:48:22 ***** | Begin main loop
	•	start: This is similar in behavior to the “mark” option, but is intended to record the start of some code block or action. Here is an example illustrating how this might be used in practice: start_time=LogRec(logbook,&quot;&lt;&lt;start&gt;&gt;Begin UpdateSafePaths @ Branch=&quot;+abs_path_string); In this example, the current time is returned from the LogRec( ) function and placed in the variable “start_time.” The log entry text after the &lt;&lt;start&gt;&gt; option provides a description of what is being started (in this case the “UpdateSafePaths” stamp) followed by a string that represents an absolute path to the currently selected Note. The resulting log record will look something like this: 	##### Start @ Index=00782 | 22-06-25 15:22:34 ##### | Begin UpdateSafePaths @ Branch=/Main Document
	•	end: The “end” option is intended to mark the end of a code block or other action where the log record will include an elapsed time value based on  calculating the time interval from some earlier starting time. This option assumes that, following the &lt;&lt;end&gt;&gt; option, the starting time will be provided in the log entry argument. Using the example above for the “start” option, here is how the end of an action (in this case, a stamp) can be recorded: 	end_time=LogRec(logbook,&quot;&lt;&lt;end&gt;&gt;&quot;+start_time); The “start_time” variable was previously captured from the LogRec( ) function. Capturing the ending time in a variable (e.g., “end_time”) is optional. This example will record a new log entry appearing similar to this: 	#####   End @ Index=00815 | 22-06-25 15:22:35 | Elapsed time=00:04 ##### Note that any user-supplied text, aside from the start time, is excluded from the resulting log record. It is strongly recommended that only the start time be entered following the &lt;&lt;end&gt;&gt; option.
Several Tinderbox stamps have been included to simplify operation of this logging facility, and to allow testing of the LogRec( ) function. All of these stamps are organized under “Logging” in the Tinderbox “Stamps” menu. 
For the stamps that do enter new log records, the log book used will be “StampLog.” This might also be a convenient log book for any other user stamps that perform logging. The currently defined stamps are:
	•	Stop Logging! This merely unchecks—i.e., sets to false—the $LoggingEnabled attribute for the “/Document Commons/LogBooks” container, which will stop/pause all logging (this is a global setting). To restart logging, go to “/Document Commons/LogBooks” and check (set to true) the $LoggingEnabled attribute. The purpose of this stamp is to provide a quick way to stop all logging should a coding problem cause run-away logging. Keep in mind that this can happen easily when debugging code that includes calls to the LogRec( ) function. Also, just because logging is stopped, does not mean that the run-away code issue has been resolved, just that the log book(s) will stop filling up. 
	•	Bookmark Entry: This enters a distinct bookmark record into the “StampLog” log book that always includes the current log index plus a date-/time-stamp. This is intended to allow a user to demarcate periods of logging activity. For example, when preparing to debug some Action code, the bookmark stamp can be used to capture when a particular debug exercise is being kicked off, and perhaps also when it ends. If the StampLog book does not currently exist, then this stamp will cause it to be created and initialized. This stamp now uses the “&lt;&lt;mark&gt;&gt;” option feature recently added to the LogRec( ) function.
	•	Stamp Log Entry: This stamp will enter a log record into the “StampLog” book using the $MyString value of the currently-selected note. This can be convenient for testing the behavior of the LogRec( ) function, as well as for making ad hoc entries into the StampLog book. If “StampLog” does not currently exist, invoking this stamp will create and initialize it.
	•	Restart Index Sequencing: Normally, the logging index will increment every time a new log entry is made to any log book. Since this guarantees that log indexes will be unique and in sequence order, the starting value of the Index is not particularly relevant. However, for those who like predictability, this stamp merely sets the Index kept in $MyNumber(&quot;/Document Commons/LogBooks&quot;) to zero, which means that the very next log entry made to any log book will have an index of one (1). However, before setting the Index to zero, a log record is written to the “PersistentLogBook” indicating that the Index will be reset.
	•	Delete Log Books: Just like the name says, this stamp will delete every child under the “/Document Commons/LogBooks/” container that has “Log Book” as the child’s $Prototype. A descendant of the “Log Book” Prototype has been defined labeled “Persistent Log Book” that can be manually assigned to a log book. This will exempt any such log books from erasure by this stamp. When using logging to debug code, this stamp can be used to quickly clear all log books when debugging is complete (or the user just wants to start over).
This “Toolbox” document has been configured to make the features of the logging tools as visible as possible. In some cases, users may want to adjust what is displayed to eliminate some of the clutter. For example, closing up the displayed attributes ([Cmd]+[Shift]+K) pane for log books will provide more room to view the actual log records. The next note labeled “StampLog” provides an example of what log records look like. 
Also, the “/Document Commons” container must be kept as the last top-level Note/Container in any Tinderbox document using these logging tools. There is a rule associated with “Document Commons” that will always force this container to be the last top-level item. The name can be changed however, since all references to this container and its children are made using relative designators—e.g., “(lastSibling(cover))”. This represents some initial scaffolding that may eventually make it easier to import packages of code and notes into documents. For the time being, the name of the “LogBooks” container should be kept as is.
However, by using agents, the log books can be made accessible from anywhere in the user’s document. This is illustrated in this Document with the “Collection Agents” top-level container. The aliases for any persistent log books will always be collected by the “Persistent Log Books” Agent, and aliases for any other log books will be collected by the “Current Log Books” Agent. 
Please direct any feedback, corrections, or suggestions to either the Tinderbox forum or directly to the author, Chuck Wade, email: Chuck@Interisle.net. </text>
