<attribute name="Subtitle" >Document Attributes</attribute>
<attribute name="Xpos" >31.4</attribute>
<attribute name="Ypos" >-0.1</attribute>
<text >Document’s User-Attribute Notes 
If $Checked is on (true), then the $OnVisit code for this container will be executed. This results in each User Attribute in the document being associated with a child “Attribute Note” within this container that will reflect the settings of the corresponding User Attribute. If an Attribute Note does not already exist for a User Attribute, then it will be created. If there is an existing Attribute Note, then the current settings for the User Attribute will overwrite copies of these settings in the corresponding Attribute Note. Normally, $Checked should be off, and only turned on when initially building the complete set of Attribute Notes, or when it is necessary to synchronize User Attribute settings with the Attribute Notes. 
The following table shows the mapping of User Attribute settings to System Attribute values in each of the Attribute Notes.

	$Subtitle	type	A string representing the attribute type
	$MyBoolean	sequential	If the type is “number,” then this option enables sequential default value assignments
	$MyString	default	Values to assign to Attribute by default
	$MySet	suggested	A list of values that can be selected from a pop-up list
	$MyNumber	lines	For some Attribute types, the number of lines to be shown in the Displayed Attributes pane is given here
	$Text	description	A user-provided description of the User Attribute. It is often easier to edit descriptions in the Text pane than using the Inspector. 
When an Attribute Note is first created based on an existing User Attribute, or subsequently updated via the $OnVisit for this container, the Edict and Rule for the Attribute Note will both be set to invoke this action:
    action($Text(&quot;/Hints/Edict «Attribute Note»&quot;));
This code will reverse the above $OnVisit, and instead update the corresponding User Attribute with the values currently set in the Attribute Note. If there is no corresponding User Attribute, then it will be created. For initialization of User Attributes moved to a Tinderbox document as an Attribute Note, the Rule can be enabled, but will run only once by setting $RuleDisabled to true upon completion. Thereafter, the infrequent Edict will cause any changes to the Attribute Note to be mapped to the corresponding User Attribute. If this behavior is not desired, then the Edict can also be disabled.
There is an additional option controlled by the $Checked attribute for each Attribute Note that determines whether only the description is updated in the corresponding User Attribute (not checked), or if all User Attribute settings will be updated (checked). The intent is to allow the Attribute Note to always be the preferred way to edit User Attribute descriptions while letting the Inspector control other User Attribute settings. However, by setting $Checked to true, User Attributes settings will be governed by the User Attribute Note, but users should be aware that changes to User Attribute settings made via the Inspector might not get applied permanently.</text>
