<text >Getting Started
Installing the Document Commons Framework into an existing or new Tinderbox document is an almost trivial exercise. This requires a source document where a copy of the Document Commons framework has been assembled as illustrated here:
￼
Merely select the red Copy of “Document Commons” container (choose the “red pill”) as shown:
￼
Then copy this selection, and paste it into the desired destination document. That’s it! 
A new blue “Document Commons” container will be established as the last top-level item in the destination document, and any intermediate materials from the installation that are no longer needed will disappear. Actually, these installation residuals will have been moved into a “Discards” container that will be the last child of the new Document Commons. The resulting appearance will be like this:
￼
If the source Copy of “Document Commons” container was assembled with “add-on” packages, then these extra packages will be installed as part of the installation procedure without any further user action required.
The “Document Commons” can be updated at any time by repeating the above procedure with an updated version of the source Copy of “Document Commons” container. This would include any add-on packages assembled as part of the source copy. 
The installation procedure will cause the “Built-in Hints” container to be established within the destination document, along with some essential prototypes and templates. By setting the Document Commons container to be a separator, this container and its contents will not show up in Map View, which avoids this becoming a distraction. 
Why install a Document Commons Framework?
The simple answer is to provide standard conventions and document structural elements that facilitate building and deploying Tinderbox Packages. A Package is a collection of notes, including prototypes, templates, code elements, user attributes, link types, and anything else that might be necessary for successful use of this collection of notes. The core concept behind a Package is that it provides portability between documents. For an individual Tinderbox user, a package could be their own personal collection of prototypes, templates, user attributes, stamps, functions, and any related notes that the user would like to be able to move into any of their Tinderbox documents. Once the Document Commons Framework is deployed within a document, the user can add further packages one-at-a-time as needed. 
A package could also be a Tinderbox collection shared amongst multiple users, or the entire Tinderbox community. Examples might be packages that implement some application within a Tinderbox document, or that facilitate exporting Tinderbox documents in various ways. There might also be packages that assemble some useful set of stamps or functions that can be used to build other Tinderbox applications. In other words, there could be packages that provide common code libraries that can be used by other packages.
Documentation for this framework and associated packages has been integrated into the various notes/containers that comprise the overall framework. All code includes extensive comments to explain the actions involved in deploying the framework elements. Each package generally includes its own documentation for the use of the package and its operational behavior.  
What is included with the Document Commons Framework?
The purpose behind the Document Commons Framework is to establish standard methods and conventions for packages to use when ported between documents. Instead of every collection of notes having its own ad hoc way of operating within a document, the framework provides consistency so that packages from multiple sources can be integrated into a Tinderbox document without conflicts or unnecessary interference. At the same time, this framework focuses on a minimal set of features that offers considerable flexibility for how packages can leverage the framework. Essentially, the framework provides a standard “place” where packages can access elements they might need. To achieve this essential purpose, the Document Commons Framework provides these core elements:
	•	Global Settings A container where data objects that are essential to package configuration and use are maintained. This can include Tinderbox notes or containers that provide shared data elements, such as counters, indexes, lists, sets, lookup dictionaries, visual themes, or common data objects. One potential use is to provide a configuration note that provides various settings used to determine how the package should operate within a specific Tinderbox document. Another use case might be to provide a shared repository for “Posters” to utilize.
	•	Document Attributes The notes in this container mirror “User Attributes” defined in the Tinderbox Document. Any existing User Attributes can be automatically mapped to “Attribute Notes” that reflect all features of the Attribute. In addition, Attribute Notes from other documents can be pasted into the “Document Attributes” container, where they will be automatically added to the destination document’s set of User Attributes. Attribute Notes can also be used to edit User Attributes, which can be especially helpful in maintaining descriptive text for User Attributes. Packages can include any required User Attributes as notes to be added to this “Document Attributes” container.
	•	Packages This container provides a repository for Packages. New packages can be assembled within this repository, and existing packages can be updated and maintained. Package automation can install or update a package within a Tinderbox document when a package is added to, or replaced in, the repository. A standardized Package Installer will check to see if items included in the Package have already been deployed in the document and will only replace such items if the Package item has the same “Modified” timestamp, or is newer. When items are replaced with a newer version, the prior version is moved into the “Discards” container (see below). In some respects, the ability to install and update packages is one of the most important capabilities provided by the Document Commons Framework.
	•	LogBooks A common need for automation is to be able to record events and information useful in debugging code. Since many packages will need to record log records, this container is the preferred location for maintaining logbooks. An essential add-on package installed with the Document Commons Framework is a “Debugging and Logging Tools” package that other Packages can make use of. Also, note that the Installers will record the actions they take in an “InstallerLog” note contained in “LogBooks.” The InstallerLog provides transparency for all actions taken by any of the Installers.
	•	Discards Whenever notes or containers are replaced or become unnecessary, they are generally moved into this “Discards” repository. This is useful if a user needs to recover something that should not have been discarded. However, the “Discards” container is intended to be only a temporary repository, and individual items, or the entire container, can be deleted at any time. Note that the “Essentials” container (see below) is moved into this repository after the Document Commons framework has been successfully deployed. When updates are installed, this container will be created automatically if it had been previously deleted.
	•	Essentials Since the “Document Commons” framework requires some data structures and code automation to support Package deployment and operation, these features are included in an “Essentials” container that is part of the installed Document Commons. An Installer is built into this Essentials container that will automatically move all the necessary features and capabilities into a document when the parent Copy of “Document Commons” container is pasted into the document. This Essentials Installer can also automatically install one or more “Add-on Packages” as part of the Document Commons installation procedure. This means that merely pasting a “Documents Commons” container into a document can simultaneously install the necessary structure along with a set of Packages as one integrated procedure. Furthermore, the same approach can be used to apply an updated “Document Commons” container to a document where this had been previously installed, with the result that any updated items will replace existing items. After the Essentials have been installed, this container will move itself into the “Discards” container (see above).
What comprises a Package?
Packages can be built by merely assembling a collection of Tinderbox notes. A standardized “Installer” will handle deployment of the collection into a Tinderbox document, including subsequent updating of the collection with new material or code revisions. However, users can build their own packages without having to perform any code development. 
Each Tinderbox Package is provided as a specially constructed container that must include a subordinate container named “Manifest.” There should also be a ReadMe note, a container for “Documentation” (e.g., usage guides, functional descriptions), and possibly a “To Do Items” container where changes are tracked as task activities (the package equivalent of the Eastgate “spike”).
The Manifest includes a pre-built package “Installer” along with containers used to collect the package objects according to type: 
	•	Package Settings Any notes used to hold configuration settings or common data elements used by the Package.
	•	Action Items Action code notes used for rules, edicts, or triggered actions. These will be installed directly in the “/Hints” container.
	•	User Attributes Any user attribute notes required to install the corresponding user attributes in the destination document.
	•	Prototype Items Prototypes used by the Package
	•	Template Items Required Templates
	•	Library Items Primarily Functions maintained in “/Hints/Library” and possibly other code modules.
	•	Stamp Items Stamps maintained in “/Hints/Stamps”
	•	Preview Items Previews that belong in “/Hints/Preview”
	•	Tagger Items Taggers that belong in “/Hints/Taggers”
	•	Highlighter Items Highlighters that belong in “/Hints/Highlighters”
	•	Link Types Currently, this comprises notes that make use of specific Link Types so that when copied into the destination document, these Link Types will be established.  Note that it is not possible at this time to configure individual Link Types as part of the automated installation procedures.
	•	Package Dependencies This merely lists the names of packages on which the Package depends. At this time, this just documents any dependencies, though eventually this may be used to enforce installation requirements.
When a package is built, any of the required package items referenced above will be copied into the corresponding container. This will normally be accomplished by using copy-and-paste procedures. 
By design, Packages themselves have as few dependencies as possible. No prototypes or user attributes are necessary for the package installation process. The entire Package container can be copied into a document as a self-contained object. 
To run the Package installer, the $RuleDisabled attribute for the Manifest container must be set to false (i.e., unchecked). The Installer will then run, and copy all items into their correct locations. If the package had already been installed in the document, then each item is checked to see if there is an existing item, and if there is, the Package item will replace that previously installed item only if its $Modified timestamp is the same or earlier than the item in the Package being installed. If the existing item is newer, it will not be replaced. If the existing item is replaced, then it will be moved to the “Discards” container, where it can be retrieved if necessary.
As a final step, the Installer will set  $RuleDisabled to true again. This will ensure that the Installer only runs once.
In the case of action code, functions, and stamps, the Installer will assign appropriate prototypes for each code type, and then set $ReadOnly to true. This helps to avoid unintended edits to code that might interfere with future updates. If a user does want to modify any Package code, then just uncheck the $ReadOnly attribute and re-select the code note. 
The Installer will log each action it takes in the “InstallerLog” under the “LogBooks” container. This log can be reviewed to see exactly which items were replaced or left as they were. Keeping with the principle of minimal dependencies, the InstallerLog records are created with embedded code that does not rely on any functions or other logging tools. However, code that is provided with a Package can certainly utilize debugging tools, including the “LogRec” function for outputting log records, since this function is included with the Document Commons essentials.
How to include a Package as an “Add-On” Package with the Document Commons installation?
The installation procedure embedded in the Copy of “Document Commons” container provides hooks to include Add-on Packages as part of the overall installation. This screenshot illustrates the structure of this container:
￼
In the example illustrated above, there are three Add-on Packages: “Diagram View,” “Network Tools,” and “Add-on Package Template.” The latter “template” is empty, so nothing will actually be installed. However, this can be used as a starting template for building new packages. “Network Tools” is included as a simple example of a package comprising just functions, stamps, user attributes, and prototypes. The “Diagram View” is an entire application that can be added to a Tinderbox document. (Note that the Diagram View requires Tinderbox b712 or later.)
When the Copy of “Document Commons” container is pasted into a Tinderbox document, there is an $OnPaste action that will be invoked to perform initial setup, including determining if the “Document Commons” needs to be created, or if there is already an instance of the “Document Commons” installed in the destination document. The $OnPaste action will pass along relevant context, and then activate the “Installer” in the “Essentials” container. The Essentials Installer will establish any necessary elements for supporting Packages that rely on the Document Commons structure and activate these elements. 
As a final step, the Essentials Installer will activate the “Add-on Packages” Installer, which will look for any Add-on Packages found in this container. For each Add-on Package, the Installer will activate a stub Installer provided with the Add-on that will find the associated Package in the “Packages” container, and then activate that package’s Installer. This procedure repeats for each Add-on Package entry until all Add-ons are processed, at which point the entire installation process wraps up by moving the complete “Essentials” container into the “Discards” container.
The entire installation process, including the actions of each individual Installer, are logged in the “/Document Commons/LogBooks/InstallerLog” text with fairly detailed explanations of any changes applied to the parent document. 
To include a Package as an Add-on for an instance of the Copy of “Document Commons” container, it is first necessary to build the Package, which then needs to be added to the “Packages” container. Then, an Add-on needs to be supplied in the “Add-on Packages” container with a stub Installer properly configured. This is mostly a copy-and-paste process with some light editing of the stub Installer to properly reference the Package. (A future version may automate this entire setup procedure for Add-on packages.)
The ability to build a “Document Commons” structure based on an automated installation procedure that supports inclusion of multiple add-on packages is intended to allow users to assemble their own preferred collection of packages that can be easily ported into any of their Tinderbox documents. Users can build their own packages, and they can incorporate packages shared by other users. Furthermore, when any package is updated, reinstalling the Copy of “Document Commons” container will update that package. Similarly, when adding new packages to a user’s preferred working set, repeating this procedure will add the new package(s) to existing documents. This approach also enables life-cycle management of Tinderbox documents that might be actively used for many years. Or, if a Tinderbox document is archived and remains dormant for some time, it can later be brought up to date using this approach. 
</text>
