<attribute name="Subtitle" >User Attribute Management</attribute>
<attribute name="Xpos" >10.5</attribute>
<attribute name="Ypos" >0</attribute>
<text >For those who only bother reading documentation after they get themselves into trouble, this is for you:
To deploy this User Attribute Management capability into your own Tinderbox documents, follow these steps:
	1.	Make sure you have the standard “/Hints” container installed in your document. Use File→Built-in Hints to add this if it is not already in place.
	2.	Select and Copy the “Document Commons” container from the “Commons Templates” container.  ￼ Important: Be sure to select the Container pointed to by the Template arrow in the above illustration.
	3.	Paste the “Document Commons” template container into the destination (target) Tinderbox document. Then turn off the $RuleDisabled checkbox for this pasted container. It will then move to where it is supposed to be in your document.
	4.	 In your target document, select the “Document Attributes” container under “Document Commons” and turn off the $RuleDisabled checkbox ($RuleDisabled will immediately be turned back on since this only runs once). Also, turn on $Checked for this container to allow User Attributes to be represented as Attribute Notes.
Aside, for this to work as expected, Tinderbox 10.1.1b704 or later is required. However, this should work with the released 10.1.0 version, though you will need to manually perform benign edits to the code text for the “OnAdd…” and “Edict…” code notes under “/Hints” in order to force them to be recognized by Tinderbox. Just adding a space and then removing it should force recognition.
That is it! Your existing User Attributes will appear as Attribute Notes, and you should now be able to copy Attribute Notes from the “Document Commons/Document Attributes” container to the same location in another document, and the corresponding User Attributes will be automatically created in the destination document. You can also edit the ‘description’ for a User Attribute by editing the $Text in the corresponding Attribute Note, which is usually easier than using the Inspector. </text>
